# Rajapart Rangadurai
Tamil title: ராஜபார்ட் ரங்கதுரை

"Go and ask the press manager whether this is how one makes a poster! They call this a poster? Does he imagine this is some penniless company unable even to pay its rent? I told him repeatedly that this company does not count pennies and that the poster must be magnificent. Is this how one prints the word 'Rajapart,' in letters the size of mustard seeds? Go! I do not want this poster. Tell him to print 'Rajapart Rangadurai Bhagavathar' in large letters."

After a moment's thought Gurumoorthi, proprietor of the drama company, added enthusiastically, "Hey! Not merely 'Rajapart.' Tell him to print 'Rajapart Raga Alapana Rangadurai Bhagavathar.'"

He sent Gate Kandasami away with the order, summoned the company manager, and reclined against a bolster murmuring, "Muruga! Kanda!" The shock of his weight made the silver tray beside him shake and scatter its scented betel powder upon the carpet. He decided to have the servant clean it on his return and became absorbed in other important thoughts.

The company had a good name, fame, income, praise and photographs in the newspapers. These were not merely words. They had assumed material form as a silver tray, a velvet mattress, and an iron strongbox. There were a hundred actors alone. Add the assistants, extras, and others, and there were another fifty. There might be fifty guests too. Thus when Gurumoorthi saw the entire company seated in rows for the one o'clock meal, he experienced divine delight. He would bring two prominent local men merely to let them witness the sight.

As he reclined, his mind's eye returned to the days when he could not find even four good actors and when the day's collection did not cover the shed's rent and the handbills. Before that he had possessed no company at all: from time to time he had travelled from town to town, gathered a few people, taught them a special performance, and staged a folk play. He had endured endless hardship between losing the name "Dancing Gurumoorthi" and acquiring the name "Gurumoorthi, Proprietor of the Bharata Mata Drama Company." As he remembered it all, the radiance of victory danced upon his face.

The manager arrived with a bundle of "Rao's" betel leaves.

"What was yesterday's collection?"

"A little low yesterday. There was a light drizzle."

"What was the amount?"

Among the important matters that had brought the manager was Rangadurai's portrait. He hurried out and returned with the handsomely framed picture. Gurumoorthi told him to hang it beside Ravi Varma's Lakshmi. The manager placed it between the pictures of Lakshmi and Saraswati. Above all three hung a large portrait of Gurumoorthi himself. Gurumoorthi looked from one to another with pleasure and said, "Look at the lustre upon our Durai's face! Marvellous. That is what people call the radiance of Lakshmi."

The manager followed this with a torrent of praise reportedly heard around town: Durai's brilliant eyes, sweet voice, knowledge of musical phrases, and acting ability. He concluded, "If Rangadurai happens to drive through the shopping street, a crowd gathers at once. They run after him shouting, 'There! Rajapart Rangadurai Bhagavathar!'"

"Yes, our Durai's name has now become famous. How could it fail to? We have put up six thousand double-royal posters, ten thousand single-royals, and seven thousand six-royals. Not a corner or crevice of the town remains uncovered. All that publicity was for Rangadurai. What wonder if the townspeople praise 'Rajapart Rangadurai Bhagavathar'?" Gurumoorthi explained at length. He felt no anger or jealousy towards Rangadurai.

"Seven hundred and sixty rupees," the manager answered the earlier question.

"Is that all?"

Gurumoorthi inwardly laughed even as he asked it, for there had been days when he had considered a collection of sixty rupees a stroke of fortune. Yet many people had already ploughed the field of his mind until a seed dropped there during this conversation could bear fruit.

"Rangadurai's heart is as pure as his voice."

"That is why he advances. We too work without counting money so that his name and fame may grow."

"One receives the fruit of what one gives."

"True. That is the way of good people. Are there not others who betray the very house that fed them?"

"Our Rangu is not of that nature. His affection for you is boundless. Only yesterday the editor of a drama magazine asked him who his teacher was, how long he had been in theatre, and in which play he first acted. See our Rangu's nobility! He replied, 'My teacher is Kalakesari Gurumoorthi. I entered the dramatic profession only through his help, blessing, and instruction.'"

"Our Rangu is a good boy, with a good heart and character. Has he even conferred the title Kalakesari upon me? His affection for us is genuine. Why disappoint him? From now on, whenever my name appears on posters, handbills, and advertisements, print it as 'Kalakesari Gurumoorthi.' It will satisfy Rangu."

After this conversation, Gurumoorthi's face blossomed at the growing takings. With tenderness towards Rangu, their principal cause, he issued fresh orders: "Give him milk with almonds; give him ten rupees for personal expenses; sew two more pillows for his bed; print ten thousand art cards bearing his portrait and sell them at the theatre for two annas each. Let Rangu's fame spread." Then he dozed.

At the time, the company's production of Kandha Leela was a tremendous success. Rajapart Rangadurai Bhagavathar played Murugan. Many other actors performed the remaining roles, but their names were unknown outside; it was not customary to announce them.

Rangu's delight exceeded even Gurumoorthi's. The reputation and influence he acquired through theatre poured honey into his young heart. Gurumoorthi's affection and concern moved him. The privileges bestowed upon him, enough to make the other actors jealous, created deep attachment to Gurumoorthi. Gurumoorthi rejoiced in making Rangu a Bhagavathar, a Rajapart, and an exponent of raga alapana; Rangu rejoiced in making Gurumoorthi a Kalakesari.

Friends who enjoyed small benefits from both men spoke and wrote: "The acting of Rajapart Raga Alapana Ranga Bhagavathar was exquisite. If one must find a fitting comparison for the sweetness of his voice and his knowledge of musical phrases, it can only be compared to modern Kalakesari Gurumoorthi's exceptional skill in running the company, discovering gifted actors, assigning places suited to their gifts, and bringing them forward with mature judgement."

Others, fewer in number, told Gurumoorthi: "Rangadurai's good fortune was to receive your support. That alone made him a Rajapart and Bhagavathar. Had you not shown him such affection, his voice would have remained wooden and without support, and he would be singing 'Rama Nannu Brovara' in some bhajan hall. His horoscope's great blessing was your favour."

Gurumoorthi would then forget the days when he struggled with sixty-rupee collections and lacked four capable actors. He remembered instead the double-royal and single-royal advertisements he had bought for Rangadurai. Proudly he would say, "Let the poor fellow advance. It does not matter if it costs us something. Whether he remembers it or forgets and grows swollen-headed, we shall do our duty."

The poison-sowers would drop their seed: "If he becomes swollen-headed, consider him finished. Without your help there would be neither a Rajapart nor raga alapana. After that he would be left floundering." Gurumoorthi's smile answered yes.

The same people would go to Rangadurai: "You do not know how it was before. People would not even walk along the street where the shed stood. The band played until eleven at night, yet only thirty or forty tickets sold. The company was perpetually short of food. What actors, costumes, or scenery did it have? Gurumoorthi's opportunity began only after you joined. Collections rose to seven hundred and a thousand. Your voice became his wealth."

Rangadurai would answer only with praise: "He loves art. I hear he has run a company for more than twenty years."

The poison cultivators replied, "He ran it for twenty years, yes, but no good artist entered. There was not an actor capable of singing two refined songs. They performed Madurai Veeran and Madasami Kamikkam to empty houses. Only after you joined came Kandha Leela and Kadambari, double-royal posters and cinema slides, cups, shields, garlands, honour, esteem, and money. Now he boasts, 'Do not regard our company lightly. The presence of Raga Alapana Rangadurai Bhagavathar as Rajapart tells you what kind of company it is. A double jasmine does not bloom on a croton plant.'"

"But what he says is true," Rangadurai argued. "Can double jasmine bloom on a croton? Had I been trapped in a useless company under an ignorant proprietor, could my acting and music have developed?"

Those practised in cultivating poisonous weeds answered, "A comparison can be made any way one pleases. Is everything settled by saying jasmine cannot flower on a croton? Is there not a pearl in an oyster and a red lotus in mud? We too can say that in the same way a Ranga Bhagavathar happened to exist under Gurumoorthi."

A smile would glide across Rangadurai's face. It said that his music had developed enough to distinguish the company and that his acting had acquired enough reputation to bring Gurumoorthi profit.

The truth lay very far from both the thoughts moving through their hearts and the claims moving across the poison-sowers' lips, whether or not those claims existed in their hearts. The important question was not which man profited from the other or through whose help the other rose. Such a question was a bubble upon water.

The true fruit of the Gurumoorthi-Rangadurai partnership was the splendid opportunity it gave dramatic art to spread through the land. The greatest benefit went to the art itself, and therefore to everyone with an artistic heart. Theatre gained more than either Gurumoorthi or Rangu. People who had paid money merely to receive itching eyes and tormented ears could now see a fine actor, enjoy pleasant music, watch cultivated plays, and find a feast for eye, ear, and thought. Their partnership was a new, excellent, and necessary aid to dramatic art.

But talk of double-royal and single-royal posters and of collections rising from sixty to a thousand grew until it obscured this truth. One party said, "Only the company made Rangadurai a Bhagavathar." Another said, "Only Rangu made the company prosper." A dangerous condition developed, and Gurumoorthi and Rangadurai unknowingly became its victims.

Rangu, away on leave in his village, wrote Gurumoorthi: "Mother has still not recovered. How are the company's collections there? I am anxious they may have fallen badly. I shall return as soon as possible. After I come, if we open Kandha Leela and run it for a month, we can make good any present loss."

Rangu expected Gurumoorthi to say proudly, "Even if the other actors spend ten years here, they will not acquire one hundredth of Rangadurai's concern for the company. He has gone home because his mother is ill, yet even there he worries about our collections and writes that we should stage Kandha Leela and that he will return quickly. Only men of such character make a company prosper." As in former days, he expected Gurumoorthi to send tonic and sweet limes.

But the letter arrived after the poisonous weed had sprouted, and the result was different.

"See this Durai's swollen pride! Collections have apparently become dull because he is absent. He writes mockingly: 'Do not worry. After I return we can stage Kandha Leela and recover the loss.' He thinks the company cannot run without him and that we have no other refuge. We made this discordant fellow, who did not know Thodi from rhythm, a Bhagavathar, Rajapart, and raga singer. The result is that he now indirectly asks us how the company can run without him!" Gurumoorthi raged.

He summoned the manager. "Finish Nallathangal tomorrow, give two days' leave, and then begin Kandha Leela. Put up posters."

Not knowing the order arose from the letter, the manager asked, "They say Rangadurai will take another two weeks to return. How can we perform Kandha Leela?"

"Are you a complete fool? Does dawn come because the cock crows? Can Kandha Leela not be staged without Rangan? Must it not? Has it never been? Is Rangadurai himself the company? Do not Somu and Damu sing before your eyes? Somu will play Murugan and Damu Valli. Yes, make it a double-royal poster, in colour. Print 'Somu, possessor of a melodious voice.'"

The advertisement appeared in the paper at precisely the time when the doctor in the village diagnosed Rangadurai's mother with tuberculosis and told him.

Rangadurai was wounded. "However sincerely I worked and cared for the company, the proprietor has tripped me at the decisive moment. By running the play with Somu even after I wrote voluntarily, is he not indirectly saying, 'I shall run the company with Somu; Damu will suffice; you are unnecessary'? He has forgotten gratitude. So many people told me the company was nothing before I came. I ignored the flatterers and instigators because I sincerely believed a good company must exist, that only an experienced man could run it, and that Gurumoorthi was that man. I laboured for that belief. Is this my reward?"

"The first crop is over and harvested; now we must sow for the second," thought the poison farmers. They worked vigorously, and the crop flourished.

Art lovers asked Gurumoorthi, "Why is the Bhagavathar not acting?"

Gurumoorthi pretended not to understand. "Oh! Do you mean Rangan? When you said Bhagavathar I could not tell whom you meant." Then he set the manager talking: "They are asking about our Rangan."

The manager poured it out: "Ranga Bhagavathar's station has risen very high, sir. He will no longer come and act for the company; the company must apparently travel to his village to perform. Ungrateful fellows! For a long time I warned our proprietor not to lift him higher and higher, that it was dangerous. He would not listen. If we staged Kandha Leela, Rangan alone was Murugan; in Rama Leela he alone was Rama; in Ramadas he alone was the Nawab; in Pavalakodi he alone was Arjuna. He gave every opportunity to that man. Now he has climbed to the top of the temple tower."

As though unconcerned, Gurumoorthi said, "Even a monkey can climb a tower. Go away! How many Rangus and Somus have come and gone in our company?"

Yet no account had been settled between Rangan and the company. Rangadurai had neither joined another company nor accepted even a special engagement.

The company did not stop. Nor did Rangu starve.

Indeed, absence from the company began to bring Rangadurai a kind of peace.

Gurumoorthi bore the great responsibility of proving that Rangu's presence or absence made no difference to the company. At first he thought of elevating Somu or Damu with publicity and filling the vacant place. Anxiety over the difficulty and outcome made him abandon the idea. Instead he advertised the company's spectacular scenery, new costumes, coloured lights, and revolving sets. He prepared Dasavatharam, in which anyone could play any role, and thus increased the company's strength and splendour.

One day Somu appeared as Mahavishnu and another as a gatekeeper. One day Damu was Krishna; the next he dressed as Rukmini. Gate Kandan suited Kamsa, but could not speak, so he merely moved his lips while Chokkan supplied the speech through the microphone. With such arrangements Dasavatharam continued. Gurumoorthi proved the company could operate without Rangadurai.

Rangadurai could prove only that he could live outside the company. He could not join another, for no other company offered the conditions he wanted. He lacked the capacity to establish a new company himself, and felt no necessity to do so.

At this stage Gurumoorthi began reciting Rangadurai's origins, accompanied by a lively orchestra of supporters:

"The company was at Katpadi. Collections were low, though we had remained there to prepare Kandiraja, not for the takings. One day Gurumoorthi went for a walk - meaning that he went to pacify a grocer to whom he owed money and beg him not to seize the company's goods. At the Neethinathan temple he found Rangu sitting and singing. His voice was pleasant. Conversation showed his character was good too.

"Gurumoorthi said, 'Brother, why remain here with such vocal wealth? Only bandicoots roam this Neethinathan temple; there is hardly a crowd. Join our company.' Rangu argued that he loved music and wished to serve art, which he could do from there. Gurumoorthi said, 'Madman! Nature develops according to its surroundings. Remain here and soon your voice will be useful only for singing Darbar raga. This place does not suit you. Come to our company.' He brought him away.

"Rangan stood with folded hands and closed mouth, offered Gurumoorthi the water vessel when he bathed, massaged his arms and legs when he lay down, fanned him when he ate, and carried his shoes and umbrella when he left for the company. By such submissive conduct he became Bhagavathar and Rajapart. After attaining greatness he imagined the company ran only because of him. Gurumoorthi has now knocked him on the head, seated him in a corner, and proved, 'Fool! See how splendidly the company runs without you. Our system is built so that it runs whether anyone is present or absent.' Unable to bear his shame and grief, Rangadurai does not show his face outside."

Thus some imagined they defended Gurumoorthi while they talked and abused Rangu. Meanwhile new actors suddenly appeared in the company and vanished even faster.

"Rangan once mocked that I could not keep proper time."

"When I played Balarama and Rangu Krishna, I knew it was my opportunity and gave him a good dose by adding things that were not even in the script."

"He entered this company expecting a monopoly."

The "other actors" now talked this way.

Rangadurai did not leave the village. His mother did not recover. His voice was no longer heard outside; there were no posters or advertisements proclaiming "Raga Alapana" or "Rajapart."

"He has been thrown into darkness," people who did not know his nature said with a little delight.

"Only now have I obtained the peace and freedom to move about openly and understand the world," he thought.

The company continued, and Rangan's days flowed on.

Some people unwilling to leave this cool, waxen state undisturbed decided to show a little fire.

"Have you heard? Rangadurai has apparently got an opportunity to make gramophone records - a thousand rupees for each disc," they told Gurumoorthi. How could he fail to grow angry?

Some good people, thinking to cool his anger and show that Rangu's nature had not changed, said, "Even on the record Rangadurai sings only your praise." They expected his heart to soften. The poison cultivators said, "What else could he sing? Only by singing that did he obtain the opportunity." Gurumoorthi agreed. They fanned this into flame and said Rangan had abandoned the company to make money from records.

"They say he received a gold disc after making ten records," a lover of rumours said.

"They set seven diamonds in that disc," said another, pretending to know its weight, price, and every detail.

"A gold disc set with diamonds! How could the fellow avoid growing swollen-headed?" Gurumoorthi thought.

"All this is because of you," said those who offered incense.

"It must be exposed," said those who incited him.

"I have been saying this all along. The moment I speak, people grow angry with Rangadurai and abuse him. Some even asked him, 'Who gave you this status? Have you forgotten him and left the company?'" said one who had never found success.

The disc was silver with gold plating. Even while recording, Rangu had thought he could perform at least part of the artistic service he had rendered in the company by giving records to the public. That very deed was called treachery.

The company continued. Rangadurai moved in a world of his own.

"Nowadays Rangadurai is always in the flower garden," one would begin.

"Perhaps enjoying the fragrance," another mocked.

"There is a young woman called Maragatham..." a third mischievously began.

"Why hide it? Say he passes his time merrily with her," another completed the tale.

Maragatham, meanwhile, would be scattering flowers in play with Mannarsami at a mansion.

Rangadurai spent his time researching ragas and advising the record company what ought to be done for the advancement of art.

"Look, there goes Rajapart Rangadurai," someone would say in the street.

"Call him Rangan! 'Rajapart,' indeed! All that ended long ago," another replied within his hearing.

But Rangadurai would remember that the same man had once sincerely said, "Other singers must beg Rangu for ragas. Who but Rangu can play Rajapart? The company itself runs on his alapana. Had he belonged to another company he would by now be a far greater musician." A smile would arise and defeat his first sigh.

The company proprietor's conduct struck Rajapart Rangadurai Bhagavathar's mind and gradually transformed it, until he came to feel that acting there brought only mental anguish and needless strain. It did not stop there. The mind naturally answers the question, "Can the company not run without Rangadurai?" with its echo, "Can Rangu not move through life outside the company?" Rangan supplied that answer not through words but conduct.

"He obtained great fame and money through the company and ran away to accumulate wealth and enjoy himself," people said to abuse him. Yet the same people also said, intending abuse, "What influence, fame, comfort, and pleasure he had in the company! After enjoying all that and possessing such renown, look what he has become. What address or influence has he now?"

They never followed their own words to the question: if the comfort and money were his in the company, why did he leave what he already possessed? The thought never came.

"Rajapart Rangadurai has descended merely to making a living. People imagined he was doing or planning all manner of things. In the end he has become a nobody. Madman! Looked at one way, it is pitiable," some said.

"He is attempting some trick or conspiracy," said others.

"Whatever Rangadurai plots will not succeed against our Gurumoorthi," said some.

"Even if he remains quiet, we will not," said others.

Rangadurai opened a betel and areca-nut shop in his village and sat there.

There was no board saying "Rajapart Rangadurai Bhagavathar's Betel Shop," but that was how the town spoke of it.

The shop gave him no double-royal or single-royal advertisements. How could it? At the same time he no longer practised Kharaharapriya or Punnagavarali. When he examined his profit and loss, it was in order.

Gurumoorthi examined the company's condition, and it too was in order.

People said, "Gurumoorthi is very capable. He trained a man called Rangadurai Bhagavathar for Rajapart. Do you know how splendid he was? Some difference arose and the Bhagavathar left. It was a small loss to the company, but Gurumoorthi is waiting for a good man and a suitable moment. When he finds one, he will make another Rangan a Bhagavathar. He can do it."

Those who had watched the plays knew that he indeed could make Rangadurais into Bhagavathars.

Somu and Damu regretted that he had not made Bhagavathars of them. But what could Gurumoorthi do? Butter can be taken from buttermilk; how can it be extracted even from rosewater? If he found a Rangan, he could make him a Bhagavathar. He could not do the same with Somu. Gurumoorthi knew this; Somu did not.

Rangadurai Bhagavathar became ordinary Rangan again. Earlier many had asked him, "Ranga, why remain obscure with such a fine voice? Join a company and you will win tremendous fame." Now they asked again:

"Rangu, why let your talent go to waste? You were a Rajapart and now keep a betel shop. If you sing Thodi, sorrow itself flees. When you sing a Kavadi Chindu, even a Vaishnavite wonders whether to become a Saivite. Yet you waste all that talent asking, 'Would you like Scissors or Wills cigarettes? Ashoka or fragrant betel powder?' Is this right?"

"You are right, but it is a large subject and I have no time to explain it. Shall I add juice to your soda, or is plain soda enough?" Rangan asked from his seat in the shop.

A photograph of him dressed as Murugan hung in the betel shop. No photograph of Rangadurai Bhagavathar remained at the company. Somu wondered what would fill the empty place. The manager hung a picture of Vinayaka there.

The company had suffered no loss, from Gurumoorthi's point of view. Rangan had suffered no loss, from his own.

Those who had stood between them rejoiced that they had made Ranga Bhagavathar keep a betel shop.

Rangan thought, "What if I run a betel shop? I worked in the company and therefore excelled there. I showed its proprietor respect and affection; therefore he supported and praised me. I conduct myself in the same way here, so people in the shopping street call me a good man. I keep the betel leaves from wilting and the areca nut from spoiling. When someone asks for soda, I ask whether to mix juice with it. I attend to the shop with all my eyes and mind. I find no great hardship or anxiety in it.

"Whether one is or is not a Bhagavathar, whether one keeps a shop or acts in plays, in any undertaking a good, capable, sincerely hardworking person becomes noteworthy in whatever work he does. That is nature and justice. Jealousy, spite, and discord cannot change it. I was Rajapart in the drama company; now the shopping street has made me secretary of the Betel and Areca-Nut Shopkeepers' Association."

His station had changed. He called it peace.

Seeing the change, his detractors mocked, "Look what became of the fellow! Rajapart now keeps a betel shop!"

Not one of them considered that although Rangadurai's loss of Rajapart status and the company's loss of Rangan might not personally injure either man, they were a loss to dramatic art, one that could not be repaired. Only the very small number who understood artistic subtlety recognized it. When they saw Rangan they did not talk much or argue. Nor did they say anything to Gurumoorthi.

The important question was not whether the Gurumoorthi-Rangadurai partnership meant profit or loss to the company. Dramatic art certainly needed such a partnership. For some reason it had been destroyed, and theatre had suffered. Only that small group understood and grieved; but it was a very small group.

On some day when an actor missed the proper note, tears would fill Gurumoorthi's eyes. On some day when someone told Rangadurai, "I went to Gurumoorthi's company yesterday. Without you the play no longer shines," tears would fill Rangadurai's eyes.

Those tears, perhaps, were the final fruit of their partnership.

6 June 1948

---NOTES---
“Rajapart” was a prominent male leading role in Tamil musical theatre; the occupational title is retained rather than flattened into “leading man.” “Bhagavathar” likewise marks a respected singer-actor.

Poster sizes such as “double royal” and “single royal” are retained as period printing terminology.

The story repeatedly describes malicious intermediaries as sowers and cultivators of poison. The full agricultural metaphor - ploughing, sowing, crop, and harvest - is preserved.

The recovered Tamil source was regenerated from all eighteen scans after discovering that thirteen PNG files marked white as transparent and therefore produced empty OCR pages. OCR-damaged words remain verbatim in the Tamil block and uncertain readings are recorded separately.
